            <ArticleTitle>Occupational airways diseases from chronic low-level exposures to irritants.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>727-35, vi</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Short-term, high-level exposures to dusts, gases, mists, fumes, and smoke that are irritating to the respiratory tract are capable of inducing asthma, the so-called reactive airways dysfunction syndrome. Such exposures, however, do not occur frequently; chronic or recurrent exposures to lower levels of irritants are much more common. This article reviews the evidence that supports the concept that low-level exposures to respiratory tract irritants can contribute to the development of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and asthma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
